# srt fork (via deps/libwebsrt/deps/srt, inherited from upstream):
# CUDT::startup() guards its pthread_atfork registration with a plain
# function-local `static bool registered` (srtcore/api.cpp) instead of
# call_once, so concurrent srt_startup() calls data-race on the flag.
# Worst case is the atfork handler being registered twice; the actual
# refcounted global init (CUDTUnited::startup) is properly locked.
# Genuine fork bug - to be fixed in the srt fork repo (maxolgi/srt,
# branch websrt), then this entry gets deleted.
race:srt::CUDT::startup

# srt fork (via deps/libwebsrt/deps/srt, inherited from upstream): its GC
# thread's CUDTUnited::checkBrokenSockets scans the CUDT blocks of live
# sockets while the receive queue's worker thread is still completing a
# handshake on one of them - CUDT::prepareBuffers lazily creates and
# publishes the send/receive buffers (m_pSndBuffer, m_pRcvBuffer, ...)
# under no lock the GC scan honors (observed in gateway_e2e_test:
# CRcvQueue::worker -> processAsyncConnectResponse -> postConnect ->
# prepareBuffers vs garbageCollect -> checkBrokenSockets, racing the
# 8-byte buffer-pointer store on the CUDT block). Both racing frames are
# srt-fork-internal; the gateway merely srt_connect()ed the socket an
# instant earlier and touches neither side. Proper fix = a fork change
# (guard the lazy buffer creation or the GC's read of it), which takes a
# fork commit plus two pin bumps (srt -> libwebsrt -> here) - deferred
# to the human. Until then suppress on the writer frame only, so races
# NOT involving lazy buffer creation still surface.
race:srt::CUDT::prepareBuffers

# srt fork (via deps/libwebsrt/deps/srt): the receiver's loss list is
# written by the lazily-spawned TSBPD thread's too-late drop
# (CUDT::tsbpd -> rcvDropTooLateUpTo -> dropFromLossLists ->
# CRcvLossList::removeUpTo) while the receive queue's worker thread
# reads it lock-free inside the loss report it builds from incoming
# data (CRcvQueue::worker -> processData -> sendLossReport -> sendCtrl
# -> CRcvLossList::getLossLength). Observed in concurrency_test's
# 50-viewer run, where TSan's slowdown sends the sessions into a
# sustained too-late drop storm (RCV-DROPPED bursts, tick overruns
# >100 ms) that normal-speed runs never reach. Both frames are
# srt-fork-internal; the gateway only drives the sockets through
# libwebsrt. Proper fix = a fork change (guard the loss-list accesses
# on the two paths), which takes a fork commit plus two pin bumps
# (srt -> libwebsrt -> here) - deferred to the human. Until then
# suppress on the writer frame only, so races NOT involving the
# too-late drop still surface.
race:srt::CRcvLossList::removeUpTo

# lsquic (deps/libwebsrt/deps/lsquic, consumed unmodified): its globals
# are init-once-per-process with no internal guard - a second
# lsquic_global_init writes globals (the enc-session ex-data index s_idx
# in iquic_esf_global_init, the conn-hash seed in
# lsquic_hash_set_global_seed, ...) unsynchronized against engines
# already running. The gateway's tests embed server AND client engines
# in one process, and WTClient's lazy lsquic_global_init
# (src/wt/client.cpp, call_once per client) can land while the gateway's
# loop thread is mid-crypto (observed: iquic_esfi_init_server reading
# s_idx, get_seed reading the seed). WtServer::Bind has the matching
# "whichever runs second re-runs lsquic_global_init" comment.
# Production is unaffected (one engine per process). Proper fix =
# process-wide init coordination between WtServer and WTClient
# (libwebsrt + gateway follow-up), or an upstream lsquic guard; until
# then suppress on the lsquic_global_init writer frame only, so races
# NOT involving a concurrent global_init still surface.
race:lsquic_global_init

# lsquic (deps/libwebsrt/deps/lsquic, consumed unmodified):
# lsquic_set_log_level rewrites the global lsq_log_levels catalog with
# plain stores, unsynchronized against a concurrently running engine
# reading it in its LSQ_DEBUG macros. The reload routes its log-level
# write onto the gateway's loop thread (reload.cpp
# SetLogLevelOnLoop), which serializes it against the gateway's own
# engine - but reload_test embeds a second lsquic engine (its
# in-process WTClient viewer), and the gateway cannot serialize with a
# loop it does not own (observed under TSan: the loop-thread
# lsq_log_levels write vs the client engine's LSQ_DEBUG catalog read).
# Same dual-engine test reality as the lsquic_global_init entry above;
# production runs one engine per process, where the loop-thread routing
# fully serializes access. Proper fix = atomic log-level access in
# lsquic itself, which the unmodified-lsquic policy defers. Until then
# suppress on the lsquic_set_log_level writer frame only, so races NOT
# involving the log-level setter still surface.
race:lsquic_set_log_level
